              %p
                And again we could again just as easily use
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intersection"} intersection
                as union, even in the infinitary axiom. That is, a
                = succeed "-algebra" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi δ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta
                = succeed "-algebra," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi σ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⋃
                          %mi i
                        %msub
                          %mi S
                          %mi i
                        %mo =
                        %mo ¬
                        %msub
                          %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⋂
                          %mi i
                        %mo ¬
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi S
                            %mi i
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bigcup_i S_i = \neg\bigcap_i \neg{S_i}
          %p
            Any and all of the above notions have been used by various authors in the definition of measurable space; for example, Kolmogorov used algebras (at least at first), and Halmos used
            = succeed "-rings." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
            = succeed ";" do
